{
  "schemaVersion": "1.0",
  "scriptId": "pct-03-comparisons-and-reference-bases",
  "version": 1,
  "sourceType": "MANUAL",
  "entryNodeId": "start",
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Percentage Comparisons and Changing Bases",
    "description": "Compare quantities using a clearly stated reference base and convert relative increases or decreases correctly.",
    "teachingApproach": "Build the model step by step, explain why each operation matches the wording, show intermediate arithmetic, check units and magnitude, then practice transfer with specific remedial feedback.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": 50,
    "sourceScope": "R.S. Aggarwal, Quantitative Aptitude for Competitive Examinations, Chapter 11 “Percentage,” printed pages 308–345. Approved scope: Teach the standard comparison (part/reference whole)×100%, including 'what percent of' and ratio-to-percent questions. Develop the asymmetry between 'A is r% more/less than B' and 'B is what percent less/more than A'; derive the reverse comparison from the changed base rather than reusing r. Include ratios, fractions, marks/amount comparisons, and percentage error as absolute error divided by the correct/reference value. State the reference quantity in every solution and check that the result uses the requested base."
  },
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "id": "start",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Percentage Comparisons and Changing Bases: Compare quantities using a clearly stated reference base and convert relative increases or decreases correctly. This lesson builds the method from its meaning, works through the reasoning and checks, then practices it with targeted feedback.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1": {
      "id": "teach_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "“A is what percent of B?” translates to A/B×100%. The quantity named after “of” is the denominator. If 30 is compared with 120, the result is 25%, not 400%.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_2"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_2": {
      "id": "teach_2",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "A percent increase uses the original value as its base. A rise from 80 to 100 is an increase of 20; relative to 80, that is 25%. The new value is 125% of the old value.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_3"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_3": {
      "id": "teach_3",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Reverse comparisons use a new base. If A is 25% more than B, set B=100 units; then A=125. B is 25/125=20% less than A, not 25% less.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_4"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_4": {
      "id": "teach_4",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "If A is r% less than B, represent B as 100 units and A as 100−r. To find how much B exceeds A, divide the difference r by A, not B. The denominator change explains why the reverse percentage is larger.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_5"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_5": {
      "id": "teach_5",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "The formulas follow directly: if A is r% more than B, then B is [r/(100+r)]×100% less than A. If A is r% less than B, then B is [r/(100−r)]×100% more than A.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_6"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_6": {
      "id": "teach_6",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Percentage error compares the absolute difference with the correct or accepted value. Error%=|measured−correct|/correct×100%. If 9.8 is used in place of 10, the error is 0.2/10×100%=2%.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_7"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_7": {
      "id": "teach_7",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "A ratio can be converted to a percent against a chosen term or against the total. In a 2:3 mixture, the first part is 66⅔% of the second, while it is 40% of the combined five parts.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_8"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_8": {
      "id": "teach_8",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "If two values are each stated as percentages of a third, use the shared base to form their ratio. 120% of C divided by 80% of C gives A/B=120/80=3/2.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_9"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_9": {
      "id": "teach_9",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "A percent-point difference is subtraction of rates; a relative percent change divides that difference by the original rate. A change from 40% to 50% is 10 percentage points, but a 25% relative increase.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_10"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_10": {
      "id": "teach_10",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "The comparison base should be named in the final statement. “20% less” is incomplete if it is not clear: less than which amount? Writing “B is 20% less than A” avoids a common inverse-comparison error.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_11"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_11": {
      "id": "teach_11",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "When values have different units, convert first. Comparing 500 g with 2 kg means comparing 500 with 2000, so the first is 25% of the second.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_12"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_12": {
      "id": "teach_12",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "A result above 100% is possible when the compared part exceeds the reference. If A=150 and B=100, A is 150% of B, or 50% more than B.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_13"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_13": {
      "id": "teach_13",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "For questions about how much one number is less than another, find the difference first and divide by the stated reference. Do not divide by the larger value unless the wording says “of the larger.”",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_14"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_14": {
      "id": "teach_14",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Check a reverse comparison with a convenient base of 100. It exposes whether the numerator is the difference and whether the denominator is the amount named as the reference.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_15"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_15": {
      "id": "teach_15",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Keep signed change separate from absolute error. An overestimate and an underestimate have opposite directions, but percentage error is usually reported as a nonnegative magnitude.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "focus_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "focus_1": {
      "id": "focus_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Apply the method to this new case. Before choosing, identify the comparison base and state the operation that matches the wording.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_1"
        }
      ]
    },
